  "limitations": [
    "Response loss was a client fault injection after actual upstream HTTP 201; not a real network disconnection or uncertain database commit.",
    "403, 503 and malformed success response checks used client response injection.",
    "Changed-address 409 was real browser HTTP via fetch; the frozen UI did not permit changing saved parameters.",
    "Same-tab sessionStorage refresh recovery only; no cross-tab, closed-tab or cross-device recovery claim.",
    "Other synthetic account showed an empty address selection instead of the original intent; restoring old cookies failed because switching Django identity invalidated the old Session. Re-login of the original synthetic account recovered the unchanged intent.",
    "Initial target test was launched after a process-stop identity check failed; that first result is excluded. Both servers were then stopped, empty schema/connections/ports asserted, and the formal target/regression ran serially.",
    "User correctly predicted 409 for same-key changed-address; independent end-to-end explanation remains pending.",
    "No Redis, RabbitMQ worker, load experiment, deployment or development data verification was run."
  ],
